/* Historia — content (English): Enlightenment & Revolution */
HISTORIA_CONTENT["revolution"] = {
  intro: "In less than a century, France goes from a kingdom of privileges to a Republic that upsets Europe. Between new ideas, immense hopes, violence and coups d'état, follow the story of an era that still speaks to our world.",
  cast: [
    { n: "Voltaire", r: "Philosopher and writer", d: "1694 – 1778", b: "A sharp pen, passed through the Bastille and English exile, he wrote Candide and defended the Protestant Calas. Settled at Ferney, he became the innkeeper of Europe. He died in Paris in 1778." },
    { n: "Marie Antoinette", r: "Archduchess turned queen of France", d: "1755 – 1793", b: "Daughter of Empress Maria Theresa, married at 14 to the future Louis XVI. Loved then criticised, tarnished by the Diamond Necklace Affair, she was guillotined on 16 October 1793." },
    { n: "Lafayette", r: "General, head of the National Guard", d: "1757 – 1834", b: "A nobleman who left at 19 to support American independence, a friend of Washington. In 1789, he commanded the National Guard of Paris and added blue and red to the white of the cockade." },
    { n: "Olympe de Gouges", r: "Woman of letters and activist", d: "1748 – 1793", b: "A playwright, she published in 1791 the Declaration of the Rights of Woman and of the Female Citizen and denounced slavery. She was guillotined in November 1793." },
    { n: "Maximilien Robespierre", r: "Lawyer, Committee of Public Safety", d: "1758 – 1794", b: "A lawyer from Arras nicknamed “the Incorruptible”, he dominated the Committee of Public Safety during the Terror. Overthrown on 9 Thermidor, he was guillotined on 28 July 1794." }
  ],
  levels: [
    {
      title: "From the Sun King to the Beloved",
      lesson: { d: "1715 – 1774", t: "The Regency and the Long Reign of Louis XV", hook: "Imagine: you are five, and one morning in September 1715 you are told you are king of France. A whole kingdom holds its breath. This is how the story of Louis XV begins.", p: "Versailles, 1 September 1715. After seventy-two years of reign, Louis XIV passes away, a few days before his seventy-seventh birthday. Nearly all his direct heirs went to the grave before him: it is his great-grandson, a little boy of five, who succeeds him as Louis XV. Now a child cannot govern. So who will hold the helm of the most populous kingdom in Europe?\n\n## A Regent, a Scotsman and a Bubble\n\nLouis XIV had planned in his will to limit the powers of his nephew Philip of Orléans. But the Parlement of Paris overturns this will and entrusts the regency to this intelligent prince, curious about sciences and arts, who governs from 1715 to 1723. The court leaves Versailles for Paris, and after the austere last years of the Sun King, people breathe again: the Regency remains in memory as a period of lightness and freedom of tone.\n\nThere remains a far less amusing problem: Louis XIV's wars have left colossal debts. The Scotsman John Law, a gambler and financier of genius, proposes a new solution: a bank issuing paper money, then a great Company of the Indies whose shares are snapped up. In Paris, rue Quincampoix, people speculate day and night. Then confidence collapses: in 1720, everyone wants to swap their notes for coins, and there are not enough. Law, become controller general of finances, has to flee. The French will long keep a solid distrust of paper money.\n\n## The Beloved\n\nLouis XV is crowned at Reims on 25 October 1722, at twelve. In 1725 he marries Marie Leszczyńska, daughter of Stanisław, deposed king of Poland. From 1726 to 1743, Cardinal Fleury, his former tutor, runs affairs with prudence: he was already seventy-three when he became chief minister! At his death, the king decides to govern himself. In 1744, in the middle of a war, he falls gravely ill at Metz. Throughout the kingdom, people pray, hold processions, watch for news. When he recovers, the joy is immense, and the nickname the Beloved sticks.\n\n## A Favourite, a War and a Penknife\n\nFrom 1745, a new figure dominates the court: Jeanne-Antoinette Poisson, become marquise de Pompadour. A bourgeois's daughter, cultured, she receives writers and artists, supports the Sèvres porcelain manufactory and protects the philosophes. That does not stop her from being the target of pamphlets.\n\nThe reign also knows trials. On 5 January 1757, a servant named Damiens lightly wounds the king with a penknife at Versailles. Then comes the Seven Years' War (1756-1763), a world conflict that pits France against England in Europe, America and India. The outcome is a setback: by the treaty of Paris of 1763, France cedes New France, that is, Canada. Voltaire, for his part, was already speaking ironically of “a few acres of snow”.\n\n## A Reign That Wears Out\n\nThere are successes, though: Lorraine, entrusted to Stanisław, returns to France at his death in 1766, and Corsica, ceded by Genoa in 1768, comes under French control in 1769. But the coffers are empty, the parlements oppose every new tax, and the king makes himself unpopular by his private life and his hesitations. In 1770, he dismisses the duke of Choiseul, his brilliant minister. In 1771, Chancellor Maupeou reforms the parlements, which scandalises the magistrates. On 10 May 1774, Louis XV dies of smallpox, at sixty-four, after fifty-nine years of reign. His nineteen-year-old grandson, Louis XVI, inherits an indebted kingdom, a contested authority and many expectations.", fun: ["The Petit Trianon, built by Gabriel between 1762 and 1768 under Louis XV, is linked to the marquise de Pompadour, who died in 1764 before its completion. Louis XVI would give it to Marie Antoinette in 1774.","At Nancy, the magnificent Place Stanislas recalls Louis XV's father-in-law, who became duke of Lorraine. It has been a UNESCO World Heritage site since 1983.","Settled at Sèvres in 1756, the porcelain manufactory, protected by the marquise de Pompadour, passes into the king's hands in 1759. Its pieces become much sought-after diplomatic gifts."] },
      items: [
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which nickname do the French first give to Louis XV?", c: "The Beloved", w: ["The Just", "The Great", "The Wise"], e: "The nickname was born in 1744, when the king fell gravely ill at Metz and the whole kingdom prayed for his recovery. He would lose this popularity over the years." },
        { type: "tf", q: "Louis XV reigned longer than his great-grandfather Louis XIV.", a: false, e: "Louis XV reigns about 59 years (1715-1774), while Louis XIV stays on the throne 72 years (1643-1715), the longest reign in French history." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which favourite of Louis XV, protector of artists and philosophes, plays a large role at court from 1745?", c: "Madame de Pompadour", w: ["Madame de Montespan", "Madame de Maintenon", "Madame de La Vallière"], e: "Jeanne-Antoinette Poisson, become marquise de Pompadour, supports the Sèvres manufactory and protects the Enlightenment. The other three names belong to Louis XIV's entourage." },
        { type: "year", q: "In which year does Louis XIV die, leaving the throne to a five-year-old child?", y: 1715, min: 1650, max: 1760, tol: 1, e: "Louis XIV dies at Versailles on 1 September 1715, a few days before turning 77. The Parlement of Paris overturns his will and entrusts the regency to Philip of Orléans." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which Scottish financier launches a bank and paper money under the Regency, before the bankruptcy of 1720?", c: "John Law", w: ["Adam Smith", "David Hume", "Jacques Necker"], e: "Named controller general of finances in 1720, Law has to flee France when his Company of the Indies collapses. The episode leaves the French long wary of paper money." },
        { type: "order", q: "Put these events of Louis XV's reign in chronological order.", items: [{ t: "Bankruptcy of Law's system", y: "1720" }, { t: "The king's illness at Metz", y: "1744" }, { t: "Damiens's attempt on the king", y: "1757" }, { t: "Death of Louis XV from smallpox", y: "1774" }] },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which vast territory does France lose by the treaty of Paris of 1763, after the Seven Years' War?", c: "Canada (New France)", w: ["Guadeloupe", "Martinique", "Saint-Domingue"], e: "The defeat by England ends the French empire in North America. Guadeloupe and Martinique, first occupied, are returned and remain French." },
        { type: "tf", q: "Louis XV is crowned at Reims, like nearly all the kings of France since the Middle Ages.", a: true, e: "Louis XV, aged 12, is crowned at Reims cathedral on 25 October 1722. Henry IV, crowned at Chartres, is the exception." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which deposed king of Poland, father-in-law of Louis XV, receives Lorraine, which returns to France at his death in 1766?", c: "Stanisław Leszczyński", w: ["Augustus II the Strong", "John III Sobieski", "Stanisław Poniatowski"], e: "Settled at Nancy, Stanisław has the famous square that now bears his name built there. At his death, the duchy is joined to the crown, as planned since the treaty of 1738." },
        { type: "match", q: "Match each figure to his role under the Regency and Louis XV's reign.", pairs: [["Philip of Orléans", "Regent from 1715 to 1723"], ["Cardinal Fleury", "Chief minister from 1726 to 1743"], ["Duke of Choiseul", "Minister dismissed in 1770"], ["Chancellor Maupeou", "Reforms the parlements in 1771"]] }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "The Age of Enlightenment",
      lesson: { d: "1721 – 1778", t: "Voltaire, Rousseau, Montesquieu and the Encyclopédie", hook: "A young man leaves the Bastille, a magistrate disguises himself as a Persian traveller, a Genevan watchmaker's son writes a book that will be burned. Welcome to the century when pens make thrones tremble.", p: "In the 18th century, writers give themselves a mission: to “enlighten” minds with reason, hence the name Age of Enlightenment. Rather than believe without discussion, they ask questions. Why should a king have all the rights? Why punish so harshly? Why not travel, observe, compare? These thinkers do not form a team: they envy one another, quarrel, but they share a taste for freedom of thought.\n\n## Three Pens, Three Characters\n\nMontesquieu, a Bordeaux magistrate, becomes famous in 1721 with the Persian Letters: two Persians visit Paris and are astonished by everything, which lets the author mock French society without seeming to. In 1748, The Spirit of the Laws defends the idea that powers must be separated to balance one another: the one who makes the law, the one who applies it and the one who judges must not be the same person. This idea will inspire the American and French constitutions.\n\nVoltaire, whose real name is François-Marie Arouet, loves witticisms, and his mocking verses earn him eleven months in the Bastille. After a quarrel with a nobleman, he leaves for England (1726-1728), where he discovers parliamentary government, Newton and Shakespeare. He then lives at the château of Cirey with Émilie du Châtelet, a brilliant mathematician who translates Newton's Principia. Voltaire writes tragedies, tales like Candide (1759), letters by the thousands. At Ferney, near the Swiss border, he receives visitors from all over Europe, so much so that he is nicknamed “the innkeeper of Europe”.\n\nJean-Jacques Rousseau, born in Geneva in 1712, has another temperament: sensitive, solitary, often in conflict with his friends. In the Discourse on Inequality (1755), he questions the origin of injustices. In 1762, The Social Contract opens with a famous sentence: “Man is born free, and everywhere he is in chains.” He asserts there that sovereignty belongs to the people. The same year, his book Émile, on education, is condemned, and his native city has his works burned. His Confessions, published after his death, open a new genre: sincere autobiography.\n\n## A Tribunal and a Tour of Knowledge\n\nVoltaire does not write only for pleasure. In 1762, at Toulouse, Jean Calas, a Protestant merchant, is executed, wrongly accused of having killed his son to prevent his converting to Catholicism. Voltaire campaigns and publishes in 1763 his Treatise on Tolerance. In 1765, the condemnation is annulled. Seeing a writer get a trial reviewed was then entirely new.\n\nThe new ideas spread in the salons, where people debate around a table, in the cafés, in the newspapers, but above all in the Encyclopédie, directed by Denis Diderot and the mathematician Jean d'Alembert. It aims to gather all knowledge, trades included: the plates show forges, weavers' workshops, printing houses. The first volume appears in 1751. The King's Council suspends the work in 1752, revokes its privilege in 1759, and the pope condemns the same year this enterprise judged dangerous. But thanks to discreet protections, among them that of Malesherbes, who runs the book trade, the work goes to its end: 28 volumes, more than 70,000 articles, completed in 1772.\n\nIn 1778, a few weeks apart, Voltaire (30 May) and Rousseau (2 July) die. Eleven years later, the Revolution will claim their ideas, and their remains will rest in the Panthéon.", fun: ["To help Diderot, Empress Catherine II of Russia bought his library in 1765, but let him keep it and named him librarian, with a pension. A fine patron's gesture!","Montesquieu is not a family name: Charles de Secondat was baron of La Brède and of Montesquieu, and it is this title he signed. His château of La Brède, near Bordeaux, can still be visited today.","The first volume of the Encyclopédie opens with a Preliminary Discourse signed by d'Alembert, explaining how all human knowledge links together, like the branches of a tree."] },
      items: [
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which philosopher publishes The Spirit of the Laws in 1748?", c: "Montesquieu", w: ["Voltaire", "Rousseau", "Diderot"], e: "Montesquieu distinguishes there the legislative, executive and judicial powers. His idea would inspire the American constitution of 1787 and the French one of 1791." },
        { type: "tf", q: "Voltaire was imprisoned in the Bastille and knew exile in England.", a: true, e: "Accused of writing satirical verses, he spent eleven months in the Bastille in 1717, then left for England (1726-1728), where he discovered parliamentary government, Newton and Shakespeare." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "In which city was Jean-Jacques Rousseau born?", c: "Geneva", w: ["Lyon", "Paris", "Lausanne"], e: "Rousseau proudly called himself “citizen of Geneva”, but his native city condemned Émile and The Social Contract in 1762, which were burned at Geneva." },
        { type: "year", q: "In which year does the first volume of Diderot and d'Alembert's Encyclopédie appear?", y: 1751, min: 1700, max: 1790, tol: 1, e: "Volume I comes out in 1751 with d'Alembert's Preliminary Discourse. The last volumes of plates would appear in 1772, after more than twenty years of work." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "After the execution of Jean Calas in 1762, on which theme does Voltaire publish a treatise?", c: "Tolerance", w: ["The education of children", "The grain trade", "The art of war"], e: "Voltaire fights to rehabilitate Calas: the condemnation is annulled in 1765. His Treatise on Tolerance (1763) becomes one of the major texts of the Enlightenment." },
        { type: "match", q: "Match each Enlightenment figure to what is linked to him or her.", pairs: [["Voltaire", "Château of Ferney"], ["Rousseau", "The Confessions"], ["Montesquieu", "Château of La Brède"], ["Émilie du Châtelet", "Translated Newton's Principia"]] },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which mathematician co-directs the Encyclopédie with Denis Diderot?", c: "Jean d'Alembert", w: ["Pierre-Simon de Laplace", "Antoine Lavoisier", "Blaise Pascal"], e: "D'Alembert leaves the direction around 1758-1759. Diderot continues the project alone until 1772: more than 70,000 articles in all." },
        { type: "tf", q: "The Encyclopédie was published without any censorship, with the pope's approval.", a: false, e: "The King's Council suspends the work in 1752, then revokes its privilege in 1759, and Pope Clement XIII condemns it. It is completed thanks to protections and discreet circulation." },
        { type: "order", q: "Put these Enlightenment works in chronological order.", items: [{ t: "Montesquieu: Persian Letters", y: "1721" }, { t: "Voltaire: Philosophical Letters", y: "1734" }, { t: "Montesquieu: The Spirit of the Laws", y: "1748" }, { t: "Rousseau: Discourse on Inequality", y: "1755" }, { t: "Rousseau: The Social Contract", y: "1762" }] },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which sovereign hosts Voltaire at Potsdam from 1750 to 1753?", c: "Frederick II of Prussia", w: ["Catherine II of Russia", "Maria Theresa of Austria", "George II of England"], e: "Frederick II, who wants to be an “enlightened” king, ends up falling out with Voltaire. The latter then settles near the Swiss border, at Ferney, at the end of the 1750s." }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "Kings, Nobles and Peasants",
      lesson: { d: "Before 1789", t: "A Society of Privileges: The Three Orders", hook: "Two men cross paths on a road in 1780: one pays almost no direct tax, the other pays for everyone. Why? Because everything depends on the order you were born into.", p: "The term “Ancien Régime” does not yet exist in 1780: it is the revolutionaries who will invent it, in 1789, to describe the world they want to leave. This world rests on an idea: men are not born equal, and society is divided into three orders, each with its role and rights. France then has about 28 million inhabitants, the most populous kingdom in Europe.\n\n## Those Who Pray, Those Who Fight, Everyone Else\n\nThe clergy is the first order, about 130,000 people: bishops, parish priests, monks, nuns. It looks after schools, hospitals, aid to the poor, and keeps the registers of baptisms, marriages and burials, which serve as civil records. It owns many lands and collects the tithe, a levy on harvests. On the other hand, it pays no regular tax: it grants the king a negotiated “free gift”.\n\nThe nobility, the second order, numbers some 350,000 people. One enters it by birth, but also by buying certain offices that confer nobility. Not all nobles are rich: a country squire sometimes lives more modestly than a merchant. But they share honours and privileges, such as exemption from the taille. The wearing of the sword is reserved to them.\n\nThe third estate is everything else, more than 95% of the population. It includes peasants, the great majority, but also craftsmen, shopkeepers, lawyers, doctors, bankers and merchants, some richer than many nobles. This educated bourgeoisie bears badly being kept from power and honours. The word “commoner” designates any non-noble person.\n\n## Taxes, Dues and Forced Labour\n\nA peasant of 1780 must give a share of his harvest to the Church (the tithe), pay the taille to the king, dues to his lord, and sometimes use his mill or oven for a fee. He also pays the gabelle, a salt tax, very unequal by province: heavy in the regions of the great gabelle, absent in Brittany. He may also be called to the corvée, unpaid labour on the roads. As salt is indispensable for preserving meat and fish, the gabelle is hated, and smuggling thrives. The most famous smuggler, Mandrin, becomes a popular hero before being executed in 1755.\n\n## An Absolute King, but Not All-Powerful\n\nThe king holds his power from birth and coronation, and governs as an absolute monarch: he makes the law. In practice, he must compromise. His intendants, sent to the provinces, apply his decisions. But his edicts must be registered by the parlements, courts of justice made up of magistrates who own their office. They can refuse, send remonstrances, and do not hold back when it comes to new taxes. The king can force them by a “bed of justice”, but this triggers conflicts.\n\nThere is an institution supposed to represent the whole kingdom: the Estates General, which bring the three orders together. But they have not met since 1614! For one hundred and seventy-five years, kings do without them. This silence explains why, in 1788, their return appears both as a solution and as a leap into the unknown: no one any longer knows quite how they work.", fun: ["Louis Mandrin, head of a band of smugglers, was broken on the wheel at Valence in 1755. His exploits were sung throughout the country: he sold tobacco and fabrics cheaper than official merchants.","The expression “Ancien Régime” dates from 1789: the revolutionaries then use it to describe what they want to abolish. People living before 1789 obviously did not speak of their time that way!"] },
      items: [
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which order is the “first order” in Ancien Régime France?", c: "The clergy", w: ["The nobility", "The third estate", "The magistrates"], e: "The clergy comes before the nobility by the dignity of its function. It owns a large share of the land and collects the tithe, but pays the state only a negotiated “free gift”, not a regular tax." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "What is the levy on harvests collected for the benefit of the Church called?", c: "The tithe", w: ["The taille", "The gabelle", "The corvée"], e: "Originally, the tithe represented a tenth of the harvest; in practice it varies by region. It pays for the parish priests, the upkeep of churches and help to the poor." },
        { type: "tf", q: "Under the Ancien Régime, the third estate includes only peasants.", a: false, e: "It also includes craftsmen, shopkeepers, lawyers, doctors, bankers, merchants, sometimes richer than nobles. This educated bourgeoisie would be at the forefront of the Revolution." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which everyday product is taxed by the gabelle?", c: "Salt", w: ["Wine", "Tobacco", "Wheat"], e: "Very unequal by province, the gabelle weighs heavily in the regions of the great gabelle, while Brittany is exempt. A much-hated tax, it is abolished as early as 1790." },
        { type: "order", q: "Put these moments of absolute monarchy in chronological order.", items: [{ t: "Henry IV's Edict of Nantes", y: "1598" }, { t: "Louis XIV becomes king, at four", y: "1643" }, { t: "Louis XIV governs alone after Mazarin", y: "1661" }, { t: "Revocation of the Edict of Nantes", y: "1685" }] },
        { type: "qcm", q: "What share of the inhabitants does the third estate represent in 1789?", c: "More than 95%", w: ["About half", "About two thirds", "About 80%"], e: "The clergy is estimated at about 130,000 people and the nobility at some 350,000, out of 28 million inhabitants: the two privileged orders weigh barely 2%." },
        { type: "match", q: "Match each institution or office to its function.", pairs: [["Parlement", "Court of justice that registers edicts"], ["Intendant", "King's agent in a generality"], ["Corvée", "Unpaid labour demanded of peasants"], ["Taille", "Direct tax burdening commoners"]] },
        { type: "tf", q: "Nobles and members of the clergy are in principle exempt from the taille, the main direct tax.", a: true, e: "This exemption falls back on the third estate. Nobles and clerics nevertheless pay certain new taxes (capitation, vingtièmes), but far less than their wealth would allow." },
        { type: "year", q: "In which year are the last Estates General before those of 1789 held?", y: 1614, min: 1560, max: 1740, tol: 1, e: "Gathered in Paris in 1614-1615 during the regency of Marie de' Medici, they lead to no reform. The royal power then does without them for 175 years." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which word designates a non-noble person under the Ancien Régime?", c: "A commoner (roturier)", w: ["A country squire (hobereau)", "A gentleman", "A squire (écuyer)"], e: "The word originally evokes someone who works the land. A commoner may grow rich and even buy an office that ennobles him, but he remains subject to the taille." }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "Louis XVI, Marie Antoinette and Bankruptcy",
      lesson: { d: "1774 – 1788", t: "A Young King Facing the Financial Crisis", hook: "He loves locksmithing, cards and distant voyages he will never make. At 19, Louis XVI inherits a kingdom that costs more than it earns. Will he be able to right the ship?", p: "May 1774. Louis XV has just died of smallpox, and the court leaves Versailles to avoid contagion. The new king, Louis XVI, is nineteen. Tall, shy, ill at ease in company, he loves hunting, reading, geography and above all locksmithing: he has a workshop at Versailles where he works with a locksmith, François Gamain. He is loyal, well-intentioned, but indecisive, and he knows it.\n\n## An Archduchess at Versailles\n\nIn 1770, he married Marie Antoinette, daughter of Empress Maria Theresa of Austria. She is fourteen, he fifteen. The marriage seals the alliance between France and Austria, long rivals. At first, the young dauphine charms. Then rumours pile up. She is blamed for her spending, her parties, her Petit Trianon, her taste for fashion and her foreign origin: “the Austrian woman”. In 1785, the Diamond Necklace Affair finishes tarnishing her: Cardinal de Rohan, deceived by an adventuress, thinks he is buying a diamond necklace for the queen, who has nothing to do with it. As for the famous brioche, the line is wrongly attributed to Marie Antoinette: Rousseau puts it in the mouth of “a great princess”, at a time when she is only a child.\n\n## Money, Sinews of the Crisis\n\nThe real problem lies elsewhere: the state spends more than it receives, and the wars of earlier reigns have dug the debt deeper. Louis XVI first chooses Turgot, an economist who frees the grain trade in 1774, but the measure raises the price of bread, and he falls in 1776. The Genevan banker Necker succeeds him, finances the state through loans and publishes in 1781 an Account Rendered to the King, which presents the finances in a favourable light. Then Calonne tries a grand plan, with a tax paid by all, including the privileged.\n\nThe American War of Independence makes things worse. France supports the insurgents: alliance in 1778, sending of troops and a fleet, victory at Yorktown in 1781, peace of 1783 recognising the independence of the United States. Revenge on England is sweet, but it costs a great deal.\n\n## The Privileged Say No\n\nIn 1787, Calonne gathers the Assembly of Notables, hand-picked figures. They refuse the plan. His successor, Archbishop Loménie de Brienne, clashes in turn with the parlements. Many French people, privileged included, now demand the Estates General. In 1788, the harvests are bad: a violent hailstorm on 13 July ravages the crops, the winter is harsh, bread becomes almost unaffordable.\n\nOn 8 August 1788, Louis XVI gives way: he summons the Estates General for 1 May 1789. He recalls Necker, very popular. In the countryside as in the towns, hope is immense. This well-meaning king, who dreams of scientific voyages and good government, has no idea he will have to face a far greater storm, one that will sweep away the throne itself within five years. In the meantime, the crowds in the streets of Paris already whisper that the king no longer rules his own house.", fun: ["Louis XVI was so interested in geography that he himself prepared, in 1785, La Pérouse's expedition around the world. His ships were wrecked in 1788 near Vanikoro, in the Pacific. It is said that shortly before climbing the scaffold, Louis XVI asked whether there was news of La Pérouse.","In 1787, the edict of tolerance gives Protestants a legal civil status: they no longer have to go through a Catholic priest for their marriages and births to be valid. It is a step toward freedom of worship.","Benjamin Franklin, ambassador of the United States in Paris from 1776 to 1785, is the darling of the salons. His fur cap and plain clothes, without a powdered wig, astonish Parisians, who snap up his portraits."] },
      items: [
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which country does Marie Antoinette, wife of Louis XVI, come from?", c: "Austria", w: ["Spain", "Prussia", "Russia"], e: "This marriage seals the alliance between France and Austria, former rivals turned allies in 1756. But many French people distrust “the Austrian woman”." },
        { type: "tf", q: "Louis XVI comes to the throne at the age of 40.", a: false, e: "Born in 1754, he becomes king at 19, on 10 May 1774, at the death of his grandfather Louis XV. His father, the dauphin Louis-Ferdinand, had died as early as 1765." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which cardinal is duped in the Queen's Necklace affair, in 1785?", c: "Cardinal de Rohan", w: ["Cardinal de Fleury", "Cardinal de Richelieu", "Cardinal Mazarin"], e: "Rohan thinks he is buying a diamond necklace to please the queen; the swindlers keep it. Acquitted in 1786, he leaves a scandal that tarnishes the image of Marie Antoinette, who had nothing to do with it." },
        { type: "match", q: "Match each minister of Louis XVI to his action.", pairs: [["Turgot", "Frees the grain trade (1774)"], ["Necker", "Publishes an Account to the king (1781)"], ["Calonne", "Proposes a tax paid by all"], ["Loménie de Brienne", "Archbishop turned chief minister"]] },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which victory of 1781, won with the help of a French army and fleet, seals American independence?", c: "Yorktown", w: ["Saratoga", "Bunker Hill", "Valmy"], e: "Washington and Rochambeau surround Cornwallis by land, de Grasse's fleet by sea; Cornwallis surrenders on 19 October 1781. The conflict greatly increases the kingdom's debt." },
        { type: "year", q: "In which year does Louis XVI, then dauphin, marry Marie Antoinette?", y: 1770, min: 1720, max: 1800, tol: 1, e: "The wedding takes place at Versailles on 16 May 1770: she is 14, he is 15. Their first child, Marie-Thérèse, is born only in 1778." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Why does Louis XVI resolve to summon the Estates General in 1788?", c: "To find a solution to the financial crisis", w: ["To vote a written constitution", "To declare war on Austria", "To name a regent"], e: "The state is in debt, the notables and parlements refuse new taxes. Only the Estates General seem able to make everyone accept an effort." },
        { type: "order", q: "Put these events of Louis XVI's reign in chronological order.", items: [{ t: "Accession of Louis XVI", y: "1774" }, { t: "Alliance with the American insurgents", y: "1778" }, { t: "Peace of 1783: US independence", y: "1783" }, { t: "Assembly of Notables", y: "1787" }, { t: "Summoning of the Estates General", y: "1788" }] },
        { type: "tf", q: "Louis XVI liked to work iron and had a locksmith's workshop at Versailles.", a: true, e: "He worked there with the locksmith François Gamain. This passion for manual work contrasts with his little interest in court life." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which difficulty strikes the population in 1788-1789?", c: "Bad harvests that send the price of bread soaring", w: ["A plague epidemic in Paris", "An English blockade of French ports", "An earthquake in Lyon"], e: "A hailstorm ravages the crops on 13 July 1788, then the winter is very harsh. In spring 1789, bread takes more than half a worker's wages." }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "Third Estate to National Assembly",
      lesson: { d: "May – June 1789", t: "The Estates General and the Tennis Court Oath", hook: "20 June 1789, Versailles: the door of their hall is closed, soldiers stand guard. Hundreds of deputies find themselves in the street. What will they do?", p: "During the winter of 1788-1789, France discusses. The king has promised the Estates General, and everyone joins in. In every parish, every town, every guild, people draw up cahiers de doléances: complaints and wishes are written there, sometimes in the plain words of a country priest or a craftsman. They ask for lower taxes, fairer judges, roads, the end of certain seigneurial rights. Almost no one asks to overthrow the king.\n\n## A Question of Arithmetic\n\nOne quarrel dominates, however: how to count the votes? In 1614, each order voted separately, and the third estate always lost two to one. In December 1788, at Necker's urging, the third obtains as many deputies as the clergy and the nobility combined. But that is useless if voting is by order! The third therefore demands voting by head. An abbé, Sieyès, publishes a pamphlet that causes a sensation: “What Is the Third Estate?” He answers there that the third is everything, that it has nevertheless been almost nothing so far, and that it asks to become something.\n\n## An Opening Under Tension\n\nOn 5 May 1789, the Estates General open at Versailles, in the Salle des Menus-Plaisirs. There are about 1,200 deputies. Each order is dressed according to its rank: the clergy in cassock or violet, the nobility in embroidered coat and plumed hat, the third estate in a plain black coat, as etiquette demands. The contrast is striking. The king speaks little, Necker gives a very long technical speech, and the deputies quickly understand that they will not be told how to vote.\n\nFor six weeks, nothing moves forward. The privileged orders want to verify the credentials of their deputies separately, the third wants to do everything together. Time passes, weeks weigh. A personal tragedy adds to it: on 4 June, the dauphin Louis-Joseph, the king's eldest son, dies at seven. Louis XVI, crushed, pays little attention to affairs.\n\n## The Third Proclaims Itself the National Assembly\n\nOn 17 June, the deputies of the third take the step: with a few priests who have joined them, they declare themselves the National Assembly. They claim to represent the nation, and no longer an order. Three days later, on 20 June, they find their hall closed. The king, it is said, wants to prepare a solemn session. The deputies fall back to the neighbouring tennis court, a building where people played a sport ancestor of tennis. Under the presidency of the astronomer Bailly, they swear not to separate before giving the kingdom a constitution. Only one refuses to sign: Joseph Martin-Dauch.\n\nOn 23 June, at the royal session, Louis XVI annuls the decisions of the third and orders the orders to sit separately. According to tradition, the marquis de Dreux-Brézé comes to call the deputies to order, and the count of Mirabeau replies that only the force of bayonets will make them leave. The king gives up. On 27 June, he orders the clergy and nobility to join the Assembly. On 9 July, it takes the name National Constituent Assembly: its mission is now to write the Constitution. The Revolution has begun, without a shot being fired.", fun: ["Today, the Tennis Court still exists at Versailles, a stone's throw from the château, and can be visited. It has become a place of memory of the birth of democracy in France.","The painter Jacques-Louis David prepared an immense painting of the Tennis Court Oath, which he never finished. A large drawing remains, in which the deputies are seen with raised arms, seized with enthusiasm."] },
      items: [
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which abbé publishes the pamphlet “What Is the Third Estate?” in January 1789?", c: "Abbé Sieyès", w: ["Abbé Grégoire", "Abbé Raynal", "Abbé Maury"], e: "Sieyès, vicar general of Chartres, is elected deputy of the third estate of Paris although he belongs to the clergy. His pamphlet is an immense success from its release." },
        { type: "tf", q: "The third estate asks that votes be counted by head rather than by order.", a: true, e: "Voting by head would give the majority to the third, helped by a few allies in the clergy and nobility; voting by order would give two votes out of three to the privileged." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which Genevan banker, minister recalled in 1788, supports doubling the number of deputies of the third?", c: "Necker", w: ["Calonne", "Loménie de Brienne", "Turgot"], e: "In his report of 27 December 1788, Necker justifies doubling the third, but leaves the question of the vote hanging, which feeds the conflict." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which astronomer presides over the Assembly at the Tennis Court Oath?", c: "Jean Sylvain Bailly", w: ["Antoine Lavoisier", "Condorcet", "Gaspard Monge"], e: "President of the Assembly since 3 June, Bailly is elected first mayor of Paris on 15 July 1789." },
        { type: "match", q: "Match each place or notion to its meaning in 1789.", pairs: [["Salle des Menus-Plaisirs", "Opening of the Estates General"], ["Tennis Court", "Oath of 20 June"], ["Cahier de doléances", "Complaints and wishes drafted pre-election"], ["Royal session of 23 June", "The king annuls the third's decisions"]] },
        { type: "qcm", q: "What do the deputies gathered at the Tennis Court swear on 20 June 1789?", c: "Not to separate before giving a constitution", w: ["To overthrow the monarchy", "To defend the king against the army", "To abolish the nobility"], e: "The oath is a break: the deputies assert they represent the nation and refuse to be dispersed. The painter David would later draw from it a great painting project, left unfinished." },
        { type: "order", q: "Put these moments of spring 1789 in chronological order.", items: [{ t: "Sieyès's pamphlet on the third estate", y: "January 1789" }, { t: "The third proclaims itself National Assembly", y: "17 June 1789" }, { t: "Tennis Court Oath", y: "20 June 1789" }, { t: "The king accepts the union of the orders", y: "27 June 1789" }, { t: "The Assembly becomes Constituent", y: "9 July 1789" }] },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Why do the deputies meet in a tennis court on 20 June?", c: "Their usual hall is closed and guarded", w: ["The king invited them to meet there", "Their hall was destroyed by fire", "The Parisians took them there by force"], e: "The king has the hall closed “to prepare” the royal session of 23 June. The deputies fall back to the neighbouring tennis court, where people played the ancestor of tennis." },
        { type: "tf", q: "All the deputies present at the Tennis Court sign the oath without exception.", a: false, e: "Only Joseph Martin-Dauch, deputy of Castelnaudary, refuses to sign, out of loyalty to the king. All the other deputies present sign." },
        { type: "year", q: "In which year do the Estates General open at Versailles?", y: 1789, min: 1700, max: 1820, tol: 1, e: "Opened on 5 May 1789, they bring together about 1,200 deputies. The third estate wears a plain black coat there, imposed by etiquette." }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "The Bastille and the Declaration of Rights",
      lesson: { d: "July – October 1789", t: "Bastille, 4 August and the Declaration", hook: "On 14 July 1789, Louis XVI notes a single word in his diary: “Nothing”. He is speaking of his hunt. Some twenty kilometres away, Paris has just tipped history.", p: "In early July, Louis XVI brings regiments around Paris and Versailles. On the 11th, he dismisses Necker, a minister loved by the people. The news reaches Paris on the 12th: people believe in a coup against the Assembly. At the Palais-Royal, a young lawyer named Camille Desmoulins climbs on a table, calls to arms and pins a tree leaf to his hat as a rallying sign. Parisians get weapons. On the morning of the 14th, they take thousands of muskets from the Invalides.\n\n## A Fortress, a Few Hours\n\nGunpowder is lacking, said to be stored at the Bastille. This massive fortress, rising to the east of the city, serves as a state prison. It dominates the faubourg Saint-Antoine, with its eight towers and its cannon. Around noon, the crowd presents itself. Negotiations bog down. A shot goes off, gunfire breaks out, and soldiers of the French Guards, gone over to the people, bring cannon. After a few hours, the governor Bernard-René de Launay surrenders. In the confusion, he is killed by the crowd: the Revolution shows its violence from the first day.\n\nSeven prisoners are found there. The fortress is thus less a place of mass confinement than a symbol of royal arbitrariness, since a lettre de cachet could send someone there without trial. In the following days, its demolition begins, entrusted to the contractor Pierre-François Palloy, who even makes a business of it: he has stones carved into small models of the Bastille and sends them to the departments.\n\n## Cockade and Night of Enthusiasm\n\nOn 17 July, Louis XVI comes to Paris. At the Hôtel de Ville, he accepts the tricolour cockade: the blue and red of the city of Paris surround the white of the monarchy. Lafayette, who commands the new National Guard, has encouraged this rapprochement. The provinces, for their part, are afraid: in July and August, rumours of brigands paid by nobles trigger the “Great Fear”, and peasants take up arms.\n\nTo calm things down, the Assembly meets in the night of 4 August. One after another, nobles and members of the clergy give up their privileges. In a few hours, the feudal regime, seigneurial rights, tax exemptions and the sale of offices are declared abolished. The decrees taken from 5 August specify what will be suppressed at once and what peasants will have to buy back.\n\n## “Free and Equal in Rights”\n\nOn 26 August, the Assembly adopts the Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen. Inspired by the Enlightenment and the American example, it affirms in its first article that “men are born and remain free and equal in rights”. It recognises liberty, property, security and resistance to oppression, and says that law is the expression of the general will. It speaks neither of slavery nor of women's political rights, which will provoke debates.\n\nThe question of bread remains to be settled. On 5 October, thousands of Parisian women, setting out from the markets, march toward Versailles demanding bread. They are followed by the National Guard. The next day, they bring the king and his family back to Paris, to the Tuileries. It is said they bring back “the baker, the baker's wife and the little baker's boy”.", fun: ["After the fall of the Bastille, Lafayette sends the key of the fortress to George Washington. It is still on display today at Mount Vernon, the home of the first American president.","On 12 July, it is said the first insurgents wear green tree leaves. According to an often repeated tradition, green was soon dropped, as it was said to be linked to the count of Artois, the king's brother: they preferred the blue and red of Paris."] },
      items: [
        { type: "qcm", q: "How many prisoners are in the Bastille on 14 July 1789?", c: "Seven", w: ["Seventy", "One hundred and fifty", "More than six hundred"], e: "Four forgers, two men judged mad and a nobleman locked up at his family's request. The Bastille is more a symbol than a well-filled prison." },
        { type: "tf", q: "On 14 July 1789, Louis XVI is in Paris.", a: false, e: "The king is at Versailles, where he receives the news in the evening. He will go to Paris only on 17 July, welcomed by Bailly and Lafayette." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which governor of the Bastille surrenders and is killed on 14 July 1789?", c: "Bernard-René de Launay", w: ["Baron de Besenval", "Marquis de Lafayette", "Marshal de Broglie"], e: "Led toward the Hôtel de Ville, Launay is killed by the crowd on the way; the provost of the merchants Flesselles suffers the same fate. The Revolution shows its violence from its first days." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which colours of Paris are added to the king's white to form the tricolour cockade?", c: "Blue and red", w: ["Green and yellow", "Black and gold", "Purple and red"], e: "Blue and red are the colours of the city of Paris, white that of the monarchy. Lafayette, who commands the National Guard, combines them: the tricolour flag will come out of it." },
        { type: "order", q: "Put these events of 1789 in chronological order.", items: [{ t: "The king dismisses Necker", y: "11 July 1789" }, { t: "Fall of the Bastille", y: "14 July 1789" }, { t: "Abolition of privileges", y: "4 August 1789" }, { t: "Declaration of the Rights of Man", y: "26 August 1789" }, { t: "Parisian women march on Versailles", y: "5 October 1789" }] },
        { type: "qcm", q: "What does the Constituent Assembly decide in the night of 4 August 1789?", c: "The abolition of privileges and the feudal regime", w: ["The abolition of the monarchy", "The confiscation of Church property", "The creation of the departments"], e: "In the enthusiasm, nobles and clerics give up their privileges. The decrees of 5-11 August will suppress most of the feudal regime, but peasants will have to buy back certain rights until 1793." },
        { type: "match", q: "Match each figure to his role in 1789.", pairs: [["Camille Desmoulins", "Calls to arms at the Palais-Royal"], ["Lafayette", "Commands the National Guard"], ["Pierre-François Palloy", "Contractor who demolishes the Bastille"], ["Necker", "Minister whose dismissal inflames Paris"]] },
        { type: "qcm", q: "What does Article 1 of the Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen proclaim?", c: "Men are born free and equal in rights", w: ["The king holds his power from God alone", "Property is abolished for all", "Catholicism is the state religion"], e: "Adopted on 26 August 1789, the Declaration has 17 articles. It affirms liberty, property, security and resistance to oppression, but speaks neither of slavery nor of women's political rights." },
        { type: "tf", q: "On 5 October 1789, Parisian women march on Versailles to demand bread.", a: true, e: "The crowd, soon followed by Lafayette's National Guard, brings the king and his family back to Paris on 6 October. From now on, the sovereign lives under the capital's watch." },
        { type: "year", q: "In which year do the United States proclaim their independence, an example for the French Declaration of 1789?", y: 1776, min: 1700, max: 1790, tol: 1, e: "On 4 July 1776, the Thirteen Colonies proclaim their independence. Lafayette, a friend of Jefferson, presents to the Assembly a draft declaration in July 1789." }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "A King in Flight, a Divided Clergy",
      lesson: { d: "1790 – 1791", t: "The Civil Constitution of the Clergy and Varennes", hook: "In the night of 20 June 1791, a heavy carriage leaves Paris quietly. Aboard are a “valet” and a “governess” who are none other than the king and queen. How far will they get?", p: "On 14 July 1790, a year after the Bastille, Paris celebrates the Federation. On the Champ de Mars, laid out in a few weeks by thousands of volunteers of every condition, tens of thousands of people from all over France gather. The unity of the nation is celebrated. Louis XVI swears fidelity to the nation and the law. That day, constitutional monarchy seems possible.\n\n## A France Made New\n\nIn a few months, the Constituent Assembly remakes the country. It replaces the old provinces with 83 departments, named after rivers or mountains, laid out so that one can reach the chief town in one day on horseback. It abolishes titles of nobility, establishes elected judges and decides to place the Church's property at the nation's disposal, in November 1789. This property serves as security for paper money: the assignats.\n\n## When Faith Divides\n\nThe Church remains to be reorganised. On 12 July 1790, the Assembly votes the Civil Constitution of the Clergy: bishops and priests will be elected by citizens and paid by the state, like civil servants. The pope was not consulted, and his ties with the Church of France are reduced. On 27 November 1790, a decree obliges priests to take an oath to this constitution. Those who accept are the “jurors” or “constitutionals”, those who refuse the “refractories”. About half the parish priests take the oath, but almost all the bishops refuse. Among the bishops who swear is Talleyrand, the bishop of Autun. In 1791, Pope Pius VI condemns the text.\n\nThe conflict divides families, villages, sometimes parishes. For many believers, the question is no longer political: it is a matter of conscience. This rift will later feed the revolts in the West. It also weighs on the king, very pious, who hesitates to sign the decree, then sanctions it reluctantly.\n\n## The Journey to Varennes\n\nLouis XVI feels a prisoner at the Tuileries. Helped by the Swedish count Axel von Fersen, he prepares his flight toward Montmédy, near the border, where General de Bouillé waits for him with troops. In the night of 20 to 21 June 1791, the royal family leaves Paris in disguise, in a big berlin. The journey goes badly: the carriage is slow, there are delays, and at Sainte-Menehould the postmaster Jean-Baptiste Drouet recognises the king. According to tradition, he compared his face with the effigy on an assignat, but the anecdote may be embellished. He gallops to Varennes and raises the alarm. The royal family is arrested.\n\nThe return to Paris, on 25 June, is made under the eyes of a silent crowd. Trust is broken: the king wanted to leave the country and join the émigrés. The Constituent Assembly nevertheless prefers to say he was “abducted” and restores him to his functions. On 17 July, a petition demanding a Republic is dispersed by the National Guard on the Champ de Mars: the army fires, there are deaths.\n\nOn 3 September 1791, the Constitution is completed. It makes France a constitutional monarchy: the king keeps the executive and a suspensive veto, but the law is voted by an elected assembly. Only “active citizens”, who pay a certain tax, have the right to vote. Louis XVI accepts it on 13 September. Soon after, Olympe de Gouges, a playwright, publishes her Declaration of the Rights of Woman and of the Female Citizen, whose first article affirms that “woman is born free and remains equal to man in rights”.", fun: ["To flee, the royal family travels with a false passport in the name of the baroness de Korff, a Russian: the children's governess plays the baroness, the queen her governess and Louis XVI a valet. The stratagem fails.","Mirabeau, the great orator of the Constituent Assembly, dies in April 1791. He was secretly an adviser to the king, which the discovery of the iron chest at the Tuileries would later reveal."] },
      items: [
        { type: "qcm", q: "What does the Civil Constitution of the Clergy, voted in July 1790, provide?", c: "Bishops and priests are elected by citizens", w: ["The pope alone names all bishops", "The Church is separated from the state", "Catholic worship is banned"], e: "It makes priests civil servants paid by the state and reduces their ties with Rome. As the pope was not consulted, conflict becomes almost inevitable." },
        { type: "tf", q: "After the flight to Varennes, the Constituent Assembly immediately proclaims the Republic.", a: false, e: "The Assembly asserts that the king was “abducted” and restores him to his functions after a few days. The Republic would be proclaimed only a year later, in September 1792." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "What are priests who refuse to take the oath to the Civil Constitution of the Clergy called?", c: "Refractories", w: ["Jurors", "Constitutionals", "Seculars"], e: "The oath is demanded from 27 November 1790. Priests who accept it are called “jurors” or “constitutionals”; the others lose their posts and will soon be prosecuted." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which pope condemns the Civil Constitution of the Clergy in 1791?", c: "Pius VI", w: ["Pius VII", "Clement XIV", "Benedict XIV"], e: "Pius VI also condemns the Declaration of the Rights of Man. He will die in 1799 a prisoner of the French, at Valence." },
        { type: "order", q: "Put these events of 1790-1791 in chronological order.", items: [{ t: "Vote of the Civil Constitution of the Clergy", y: "12 July 1790" }, { t: "Festival of the Federation, Champ de Mars", y: "14 July 1790" }, { t: "The clergy's oath is demanded", y: "27 November 1790" }, { t: "King's flight, arrested at Varennes", y: "20-21 June 1791" }, { t: "Louis XVI accepts the Constitution", y: "13 September 1791" }] },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which postmaster recognises Louis XVI and raises the alarm in June 1791?", c: "Jean-Baptiste Drouet", w: ["Marquis de Bouillé", "Count of Provence", "Duke of Orléans"], e: "Drouet gets ahead of the berlin on horseback and has the royal family stopped at Varennes-en-Argonne. General de Bouillé, who was waiting for the king with troops, arrives too late." },
        { type: "year", q: "In which year does Olympe de Gouges publish her Declaration of the Rights of Woman and of the Female Citizen?", y: 1791, min: 1730, max: 1830, tol: 1, e: "She demands for women the same rights as for men. Guillotined in November 1793 for her political writings, she is today a figure of feminism." },
        { type: "match", q: "Match each term or figure to what describes it.", pairs: [["Mirabeau", "Great orator who died in April 1791"], ["Assignat", "Paper backed by national property"], ["Axel von Fersen", "Queen's friend, organises the flight"], ["Talleyrand", "Bishop of Autun who takes the oath"]] },
        { type: "tf", q: "In 1790, France is divided into departments that replace the old provinces.", a: true, e: "The 83 departments bear names of rivers or mountains. They are designed so that, in principle, one can reach the chief town within a day's horse ride." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which regime does the Constitution of 1791 establish in France?", c: "A constitutional monarchy", w: ["An absolute monarchy", "A republic", "A military dictatorship"], e: "The king keeps the executive power and a suspensive veto, but the law is voted by an elected assembly. Suffrage is based on tax: only “active citizens” vote." }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "Valmy, the Tuileries, the King's End",
      lesson: { d: "1792 – January 1793", t: "War, the Fall of the Monarchy and the Death of Louis XVI", hook: "April 1792, Strasbourg. In the night, a young captain writes a war song for the army of the Rhine. He has no idea he has just composed the anthem of France.", p: "In spring 1792, Europe watches the Revolution with unease. Émigré nobles are agitating abroad, and the sovereigns of Austria and Prussia threaten. In Paris, everyone has reasons to want war. The king hopes a defeat will give him back his powers. The Girondins, brilliant deputies coming mostly from the provinces, want to spread liberty in Europe. On 20 April 1792, France declares war on Austria.\n\n## The Song of Strasbourg\n\nThe beginnings are disastrous: the army is disorganised, noble officers go over to the enemy. Meanwhile, at Strasbourg, Rouget de Lisle, an engineer captain, composes the “War Song for the Army of the Rhine”. The volunteers from Marseille sing it on their way up to Paris, and the song becomes the Marseillaise. It will be the national anthem in 1795.\n\n## The Monarchy Collapses\n\nAt the end of July, the duke of Brunswick, head of the Prussian and Austrian armies, publishes a manifesto threatening Paris with military execution if the royal family is mistreated. The effect is the opposite of what was hoped: it is seen as proof that the king is in league with foreigners. On 10 August, Parisians and the fédérés storm the Tuileries palace. The king has taken refuge at the Assembly; hundreds of Swiss Guards are killed. Louis XVI is suspended then locked in the Temple tower. In September, prisoners are massacred in the prisons of Paris, in the panic of invasion.\n\nThe Temple, where the royal family is now locked up, is a former estate of the Knights of the Temple order, dominated by a big medieval tower. The prisoners live there under close watch, but keep a few habits: Louis XVI, who loves books, gives lessons to his son, little Louis-Charles. Outside, meanwhile, the news worsens the anxiety: the enemy has taken strongholds like Longwy and Verdun, and Paris fears the arrival of foreign armies. When the trial comes, in December, Louis XVI will be separated from his family.\n\n## Valmy, the Victory of the Cannonade\n\nMeanwhile, the Prussians advance. On 20 September 1792, near the village of Valmy, in Champagne, the army of Kellermann and Dumouriez stops them in a battle mostly of artillery. According to witnesses, the soldiers cry “Long live the nation!”. The Prussians withdraw. That day, the National Convention meets, and it abolishes the monarchy the next day. In November, Dumouriez wins the victory of Jemappes, in Belgium.\n\n## The King's Trial\n\nA hiding place discovered at the Tuileries, the iron chest, contains papers that compromise Louis XVI. The Convention decides to try him itself, from December. He is defended by Malesherbes, a former minister, and two other lawyers. Accused of conspiring against liberty, he is sentenced to death by a narrow majority. On 21 January 1793, he climbs the scaffold, place de la Révolution, today place de la Concorde. Drums drown his voice when he tries to speak to the crowd.\n\nThis death causes a shock in Europe: in the following weeks, England, Spain and other countries enter the war against France. The Republic, born in urgency, must now defend itself on all its borders.", fun: ["The guillotine owes its name to Doctor Guillotin, who only wanted a more humane death, identical for all. The machine was designed by the surgeon Antoine Louis and was first called “la Louisette”. Its first victim was a thief, in April 1792.","Goethe, who was at Valmy in the Prussian army, later said he told his comrades: “From this place and this day begins a new epoch in the history of the world.” He wrote it long after the events, in an account published in 1822."] },
      items: [
        { type: "qcm", q: "Against which power does France first declare war in April 1792?", c: "Habsburg Austria", w: ["England", "Spain", "Russia"], e: "War is declared on 20 April on the “king of Bohemia and Hungary”. The king and the Girondins want it for opposite reasons; Prussia joins Austria soon after." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "In which city does Rouget de Lisle compose in April 1792 the song that will become the Marseillaise?", c: "Strasbourg", w: ["Marseille", "Paris", "Metz"], e: "An engineer captain, he titles it “War Song for the Army of the Rhine”. The song takes the name Marseillaise after being taken up by the Marseille fédérés, and becomes the national anthem in 1795." },
        { type: "tf", q: "The battle of Valmy is a serious defeat for the French army.", a: false, e: "Valmy is a defensive victory: the Prussians retreat without really attacking, which saves the young Republic and opens the way to the conquest of Belgium." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "What does the duke of Brunswick's manifesto threaten if the royal family is mistreated?", c: "To hand Paris over to military execution", w: ["The blockade of French ports", "The annexation of Alsace", "The king's permanent exile"], e: "Dated 25 July 1792 and drafted by an émigré, the text has the opposite effect to that expected: it makes Louis XVI look like an accomplice of foreigners and hastens the fall of the monarchy." },
        { type: "order", q: "Put these events of 1792-1793 in chronological order.", items: [{ t: "France declares war on Austria", y: "20 April 1792" }, { t: "Storming of the Tuileries palace", y: "10 August 1792" }, { t: "Victory of Valmy", y: "20 September 1792" }, { t: "The Convention abolishes the monarchy", y: "21 September 1792" }, { t: "Execution of Louis XVI", y: "21 January 1793" }] },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which Parisian palace is stormed on 10 August 1792?", c: "The Tuileries palace", w: ["The château of Versailles", "The Louvre palace", "The Luxembourg palace"], e: "The king takes refuge with his family at the Assembly before the assault. Several hundred Swiss Guards are killed, and the Assembly suspends Louis XVI from his functions." },
        { type: "match", q: "Match each figure to his role.", pairs: [["Dumouriez", "Victor of Jemappes in 1792"], ["Goethe", "German poet present at Valmy"], ["Doctor Guillotin", "Advocates a more humane execution"], ["Malesherbes", "King's defender, former minister"]] },
        { type: "tf", q: "Louis XVI is tried by the National Convention rather than by an ordinary court.", a: true, e: "After the discovery of the iron chest, a hiding place of compromising papers at the Tuileries, the Convention decides to try him itself, from December 1792." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which general commands at Valmy, alongside Dumouriez, the army that stops the Prussians?", c: "Kellermann", w: ["Hoche", "Jourdan", "Masséna"], e: "On 20 September, after a cannonade, the Prussians withdraw: the victory is above all moral. According to witnesses, the soldiers cry “Long live the nation!”." },
        { type: "year", q: "In which year is the guillotine first used on a condemned man?", y: 1792, min: 1700, max: 1820, tol: 1, e: "On 25 April 1792, the thief Nicolas Jacques Pelletier is executed at the place de Grève. The machine, perfected by the surgeon Antoine Louis, is meant to ensure a quick death identical for all." }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "Terror, Vendée and the New Calendar",
      lesson: { d: "1793 – 1794", t: "Terror, Vendée and Republican Calendar", hook: "In a country invaded, at war with Europe and torn from within, the Republic decides to act without pity. How did a revolution born of hope tip into fear?", p: "In spring 1793, the young Republic wavers. It is at war with a large part of Europe, General Dumouriez goes over to the enemy, prices soar, and the west of the country rises up. In March, the Convention orders the raising of 300,000 men. In the West, many peasants, already wounded by the religious quarrel, refuse to go: this is the start of the war in the Vendée, where the insurgents form a “Catholic and Royal Army”.\n\n## A Government of Exception\n\nTo hold on, the Convention sets up emergency institutions: a Revolutionary Tribunal, and above all the Committee of Public Safety, twelve members renewed each month, on which sit Carnot, Saint-Just, Couthon, Barère and, from July 1793, Robespierre. In June, the Girondins are driven out of the Convention. On 13 July, Marat, a revolutionary journalist, is stabbed in his bath by Charlotte Corday, a young woman from Caen. She is guillotined four days later. The painter David immortalises the scene in “The Death of Marat”.\n\nOn 23 August, the levée en masse mobilises the whole nation: the young fight, married men forge weapons, women sew tents and clothes, children make lint. In September, the Terror is “placed on the order of the day”. The Law of Suspects of 17 September allows the arrest of anyone suspected of hostility to the Revolution. A “maximum” caps prices. Louis XVI is already dead; Marie Antoinette, tried in October, is guillotined on the 16th, then Girondins, and many others. In all, the victims number in the tens of thousands, tried or not, across the country.\n\n## The Vendée: A Fratricidal War\n\nIn the Vendée, the Catholic and Royal Army first has successes, but it is beaten at the end of 1793. The repression of the winter of 1793-1794 is terrible: civilians are massacred, villages burned. The exact toll remains debated among historians. This civil war will long leave opposing memories in the West.\n\n## A New Time\n\nThe Revolution also wants to change time. On 5 October 1793, the Convention adopts the Republican calendar. Year I begins retroactively on 22 September 1792, the day the Republic was proclaimed. The year has twelve months of thirty days, plus five or six complementary days. The poet Fabre d'Églantine invents their names, drawn from nature: vendémiaire for the grape harvest, brumaire for the mists, germinal for germination, messidor for harvests, thermidor for heat. The seven-day week disappears in favour of the ten-day décade, with décadi as the day of rest. Peasants, who rest one day in ten instead of one in seven, appreciate it little. The calendar will be abandoned by Napoleon on 1 January 1806.\n\nAt the same time, it is decided to replace the old weights and measures with a decimal system based on the metre. Another great change takes place overseas: in Saint-Domingue, the insurgent slaves and the Republic's commissioners proclaim freedom in 1793, and on 4 February 1794 (16 Pluviôse Year II), the Convention abolishes slavery in all French colonies and grants citizenship to all men without distinction of colour. Bonaparte will restore slavery in 1802; it will be definitively abolished only in 1848.\n\nIn this tense France, a new cult is also sought. Churches are closed, festivals of Reason organised, processions banned. Robespierre, for his part, prefers a cult of the Supreme Being, which he has celebrated on 8 June 1794. It will be one of the last great moments of his power.", fun: ["Marat suffered from a skin disease that itched: he spent hours in a bath, a board laid across serving him as a desk. That is where Charlotte Corday found him.","The metre, adopted to replace the measures of the Ancien Régime, was defined as one ten-millionth of a quarter of the Earth's meridian. For this, the astronomers Delambre and Méchain measured the arc from Dunkirk to Barcelona.","Each season of the Republican calendar has its ending: -aire for autumn (brumaire), -ôse for winter (nivôse), -al for spring (germinal) and -idor for summer (thermidor)."] },
      items: [
        { type: "qcm", q: "Who assassinates Marat in his bath, in July 1793?", c: "Charlotte Corday", w: ["Olympe de Gouges", "Madame Roland", "Théroigne de Méricourt"], e: "Corday, from Caen and close to the Girondins, stabs him under the pretext of delivering information. Guillotined on 17 July, she inspires David's painting “The Death of Marat”." },
        { type: "tf", q: "The Terror begins after the fall of Robespierre.", a: false, e: "It is launched in 1793 and intensifies in 1794. It is the fall of Robespierre, in July 1794, that ends it." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which body of the Convention, on which Robespierre sits, directs the government's action during the Terror?", c: "The Committee of Public Safety", w: ["The Council of Elders", "The Paris Commune", "The Legislative Assembly"], e: "Made up of twelve members renewed each month, it also includes Carnot, Saint-Just, Couthon and Barère. It centralises the army, diplomacy and the war economy." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which decision of the Convention triggers the Vendée insurrection in March 1793?", c: "The levy of 300,000 men", w: ["The execution of Marie Antoinette", "The abolition of tithes", "The proclamation of the Republic"], e: "Many peasants of the West refuse to go to war, already irritated by the Civil Constitution of the Clergy. The insurgents form the “Catholic and Royal Army”." },
        { type: "order", q: "Put these events of 1793-1794 in chronological order.", items: [{ t: "Uprising in the Vendée", y: "March 1793" }, { t: "Marat is assassinated by Corday", y: "13 July 1793" }, { t: "Law of Suspects", y: "17 September 1793" }, { t: "Execution of Marie Antoinette", y: "16 October 1793" }, { t: "Festival of the Supreme Being", y: "8 June 1794" }] },
        { type: "qcm", q: "What is the first month of the Republican calendar called?", c: "Vendémiaire", w: ["Brumaire", "Germinal", "Messidor"], e: "Its name evokes the grape harvest. The month names are drawn from the seasons and farm work: brumaire for the mists, germinal for germination, messidor for harvests." },
        { type: "match", q: "Match each figure to what marks him.", pairs: [["Carnot", "Organiser of victory"], ["Saint-Just", "Young orator close to Robespierre"], ["Hébert", "Journalist of Le Père Duchesne"], ["Fabre d'Églantine", "Poet who invents the month names"]] },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which measure of August 1793 puts the whole nation at the service of the war?", c: "The levée en masse", w: ["The General Maximum", "The Great Fear", "The Le Chapelier law"], e: "The decree of 23 August provides that the young will go to fight, married men will make weapons and women tents and clothes. The Republic's army thus becomes one of the largest in Europe." },
        { type: "tf", q: "The Republican calendar replaces the seven-day week with a ten-day décade.", a: true, e: "The last day, décadi, becomes the day of rest: one day in ten instead of one in seven, which displeases many. Napoleon abandons the calendar on 1 January 1806." },
        { type: "year", q: "In which year does the Convention abolish slavery in the French colonies?", y: 1794, min: 1730, max: 1810, tol: 1, e: "The decree of 16 Pluviôse Year II (4 February 1794) grants citizenship to all men in the colonies, without distinction of colour. Restored in 1802, slavery will be definitively abolished only in 1848." }
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "From Thermidor to Bonaparte",
      lesson: { d: "1794 – 1799", t: "The Fall of Robespierre, the Directory and 18 Brumaire", hook: "27 July 1794: Robespierre wants to speak at the Convention, but shouts prevent him. Within hours, the most feared man in France is arrested. Then a young general enters the stage.", p: "In spring 1794, the Terror has turned against the revolutionaries themselves. Danton, the great orator, and Camille Desmoulins, who asked for more clemency, are guillotined on 5 April. A few weeks later, the law of 22 Prairial speeds up the Revolutionary Tribunal's judgments, and executions multiply in Paris. Many deputies wonder who will be next.\n\n## 9 Thermidor\n\nOn 26 July (8 Thermidor), Robespierre gives a long speech denouncing traitors, without naming them. All feel threatened. The next day, 9 Thermidor Year II (27 July 1794), deputies like Tallien, Barras and Fouché get ahead of him: they prevent him from speaking and have him arrested, along with Saint-Just and Couthon. The Paris Commune tries to free them, in vain. Robespierre, wounded in the jaw, is guillotined the next day with about twenty close associates. The Terror ends. The prisons open, the “gilded youth” chases the Jacobins, and post-Terror Paris rediscovers the pleasure of living.\n\n## A Fragile Regime\n\nIn 1795, the Convention drafts a new Constitution. The Directory, installed on 2 November 1795, rests on five directors and two assemblies, the Council of Five Hundred and the Council of Elders. The executive is deliberately shared to prevent one man from taking too much power. But the regime is weak: money is lacking, the assignat has collapsed, royalists and Jacobins plot. To stay in place, the Directory annuls elections and leans on the army. In this Paris, fashion becomes extravagant: the “Merveilleuses” and “Incroyables” dress in light and eccentric ways. Joséphine de Beauharnais, an elegant widow, shines there.\n\n## A General Named Bonaparte\n\nBorn in Corsica in 1769, Napoleon Bonaparte, an artillery officer, makes himself noticed in 1795 by putting down a royalist insurrection in Paris, on Barras's orders. He marries Joséphine in 1796. That year, he takes command of the army of Italy, wins several victories, and imposes on Austria the treaty of Campo Formio (October 1797), signed without waiting for orders from Paris. The Directory, worried by his popularity, sends him to Egypt in 1798 to threaten the British. He takes many scholars with him. The French fleet is destroyed at Aboukir by Admiral Nelson on 1 August 1798. In July 1799, an officer discovers near Rosetta a stone inscribed in three scripts: it will allow Champollion to decipher the hieroglyphs, in 1822.\n\n## Brumaire\n\nBonaparte returns to France in October 1799, leaving his army. Abbé Sieyès, become a director, looks for a general to change the Constitution. On 18 Brumaire Year VIII (9 November 1799), the Directory is overthrown. The next day, at Saint-Cloud, the deputies protest; soldiers drive them out. Three consuls are named: Bonaparte, Sieyès and Roger Ducos. Bonaparte soon becomes First Consul. In December, he proclaims: “The Revolution is fixed to the principles that began it. It is over.” It lasted ten years, but it changed France forever.", fun: ["Joséphine was actually named Marie-Josèphe-Rose and was born in Martinique. Her family called her Rose: according to tradition, it was Bonaparte who preferred the name Joséphine.","The obelisk of the Place de la Concorde, brought from Luxor in 1836, stands where Louis XVI and Marie Antoinette were guillotined. It was given to France by Egypt, which Bonaparte's expedition had revealed to all Europe.","The Rosetta Stone carries a decree of 196 BC in honour of King Ptolemy V, written in hieroglyphs, demotic and Greek. Passed to the British in 1801, it is displayed in the British Museum."] },
      items: [
        { type: "qcm", q: "How many directors does the Directory have?", c: "Five", w: ["Three", "Seven", "Twelve"], e: "Each year, one of them is renewed. This collective executive power is designed to prevent a single man from concentrating power, like Robespierre." },
        { type: "tf", q: "At 18 Brumaire, Bonaparte has himself proclaimed emperor of the French.", a: false, e: "He becomes one of the three consuls then First Consul. He will be crowned emperor only in December 1804." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which director, a former abbé, prepares the coup of 18 Brumaire with Bonaparte?", c: "Emmanuel-Joseph Sieyès", w: ["Paul Barras", "Lazare Carnot", "Jean-François Reubell"], e: "Sieyès seeks a general to revise the Constitution; he chooses Bonaparte, who quickly takes the lead role. Sieyès himself settles for the post of provisional consul, then the Senate." },
        { type: "order", q: "Put these events of 1794-1799 in chronological order.", items: [{ t: "Execution of Danton", y: "5 April 1794" }, { t: "Fall of Robespierre", y: "27 July 1794" }, { t: "Start of the Italian campaign", y: "1796" }, { t: "Bonaparte leaves for Egypt", y: "May 1798" }, { t: "Coup of 18 Brumaire", y: "9 November 1799" }] },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which stone, discovered in 1799 during the Egyptian expedition, will allow Champollion to decipher the hieroglyphs?", c: "The Rosetta Stone", w: ["The Stele of Hammurabi", "The Narmer Palette", "The Luxor obelisk"], e: "Found in July 1799 by Captain Bouchard, it carries the same text in hieroglyphs, demotic and Greek. Seized by the English in 1801, it is today in the British Museum." },
        { type: "match", q: "Match each figure to his or her role under the Directory.", pairs: [["Gracchus Babeuf", "Conspiracy of the Equals (1796)"], ["Barras", "Director, protector of young Bonaparte"], ["Joséphine de Beauharnais", "Bonaparte's wife in 1796"], ["Fouché", "Minister of Police in 1799"]] },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which British admiral destroys the French fleet at Aboukir in August 1798, cutting off the army in Egypt?", c: "Horatio Nelson", w: ["Arthur Wellesley", "James Cook", "Edward Pellew"], e: "On 1 August 1798, Nelson surprises Brueys's fleet at anchor: the flagship L'Orient explodes. Bonaparte, cut off from France, nevertheless returns in October 1799, leaving his army." },
        { type: "tf", q: "Robespierre is guillotined the day after his arrest at the Convention, without trial.", a: true, e: "Outlawed, he is executed on 28 July 1794 with Saint-Just, Couthon and about twenty associates. His jaw, broken by a gunshot, was shot either by himself or by a gendarme: we do not know." },
        { type: "qcm", q: "Which treaty of October 1797, signed with Austria, crowns Bonaparte's Italian campaign?", c: "Campo Formio", w: ["Lunéville", "Amiens", "Tilsit"], e: "Negotiated by Bonaparte without waiting for orders from Paris, it recognises French gains in Belgium and on the left bank of the Rhine, and cedes Venice to Austria." },
        { type: "year", q: "In which year does the Directory replace the Convention?", y: 1795, min: 1720, max: 1820, tol: 1, e: "The Constitution of Year III is voted in August 1795; the Directory is installed on 2 November 1795 (11 Brumaire Year IV) and will last four years." }
      ]
    }
  ]
};
